      "textEn": "619\n\nIn this world, there are five elements—Earth, Water, Fire, Air, and Space. But the body is neither\ntheir reflection, nor their combination, nor their transformation. Because the body and its parts are neither\nindividual nor mixed, therefore all such principles are baseless. Meaning: The forest is certainly a disorder of the earth, but one tree is not dependent on another. If one of them is cut, it does not mean that the other tree is also cut. Therefore,\nthe jungle is neither a combination of trees, nor a disorder. Its best explanation has been given by Krishna himself—\n\nMaya, this entire universe, is the unmanifest form. I am not situated among all beings in their places. “This entire universe is pervaded by my unmanifest form. All beings are in me, but I am not in them.”\n(Bhagavad Gita 9.4) Everything in the universe is a expansion of Krishna’s energy. As it has been said—Parasyam—Shaktirvividhaive—God has various powers, which are expressed in various ways.\nThe powers exist, and God also exists simultaneously. Because every object is their power.\nTherefore, they are simultaneously inseparable from every object and also different from every object. Thus, the theory conceived by us\nThat the soul is a combination of matter and matter is a transformation of the soul, or that\nThe body is a part of the soul, is baseless. Because all the powers of God are present together, therefore man should understand\nGod. Although they are every object, they are not present in every object. Worship of God\nShould be done in their original Krishna form. They can also remain in the form of a part of their various extensive powers. When we worship the idol of God in the temple, then this idol\nseems to be made of stone or wood. But since God's body is not physical, therefore they are neither stone\nnor wood; yet stone and wood are not different from them.\nBy worshiping stone or wood in this way,\nwe do not receive any fruit, but when stone or wood is considered a representation of the original form of God,\nby worshiping that archaivigraha, we receive the desired fruit."
